WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.420
Chapter one used matched controls to identify an opsin-linked effect.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.640
When that effect looks large, how much evidence is enough?

3
00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:16.853
An experiment can record hundreds of responses without having hundreds of

4
00:00:16.853 --> 00:00:17.913
independent units.

5
00:00:19.193 --> 00:00:23.533
Repeated trials share each animal's expression, fibre placement and state.

6
00:00:24.893 --> 00:00:27.073
More trials sharpen that animal's mean.

7
00:00:28.313 --> 00:00:29.753
They do not create more animals.

8
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.113
Eight mice each complete 40 trials.

9
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.653
Does the study have 8 independent units, or 320?

10
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.440
In this design the independent unit is the animal.

11
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.420
Keep its trials nested inside it during analysis.

12
00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.380
Treating every trial as independent erases shared variation within mice.

13
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.540
The result then looks more precise than the experiment really is.

14
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:08.413
For a two-group planning example, a standardized effect divides the difference

15
00:01:08.413 --> 00:01:10.613
between the means by the pooled spread.

16
00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:15.253
The same raw shift looks larger when outcomes cluster tightly.

17
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:18.793
It looks smaller when the animals vary widely.

18
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:24.867
Planning asks which effect is worth detecting, not which effect a small pilot

19
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:30.847
happened to show. On a computed sample-size curve, smaller target effects demand

20
00:01:30.847 --> 00:01:31.927
much larger groups.

21
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:37.527
The curve rises sharply because noisy overlap is harder to distinguish.

22
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.350
Small pilots give noisy effect estimates.

23
00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.050
The pilots that overshoot are also the most tempting to follow.

24
00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:52.250
If planning copies that lucky estimate, the confirmatory study can be too small

25
00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.250
before it begins.

26
00:01:54.233 --> 00:01:59.413
The same discipline applies to human imaging, where many brain-wide associations

27
00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:00.893
among people are small.

28
00:02:02.153 --> 00:02:04.933
A blood-oxygenation signal is not a spike count.

29
00:02:06.213 --> 00:02:10.573
A group average also does not guarantee stable rankings of individuals.

30
00:02:11.783 --> 00:02:14.583
Precision and reliability are different checks.

31
00:02:15.943 --> 00:02:19.643
A larger sample narrows uncertainty around an association.

32
00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:24.723
Repeating the measurement asks whether people keep similar relative scores.

33
00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:30.943
A strong group average cannot substitute for either check when the goal is an

34
00:02:30.943 --> 00:02:32.223
individual biomarker.

35
00:02:33.183 --> 00:02:34.763
Keep three claims separate.

36
00:02:36.603 --> 00:02:39.423
Association says two measures vary together in a sample.

37
00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:44.303
Held-out prediction asks whether a model travels to new people.

38
00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:49.263
Causation asks what changes after the proposed cause is changed.

39
00:02:50.643 --> 00:02:53.443
Prediction alone does not supply that intervention.

40
00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:59.753
So size the study for the effect that matters, keep observations inside their

41
00:02:59.753 --> 00:03:02.433
independent units, and report uncertainty.

42
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:05.953
Then match the final verb to the design.

43
00:03:07.233 --> 00:03:10.113
A precise association is still an association.

44
00:03:11.413 --> 00:03:15.933
A controlled intervention can support a causal claim within its tested scope.

45
00:03:16.813 --> 00:03:19.593
Match the claim to the contrast and the sample.
